  "pop_behind_why_none": "cutout law 16 keys the crossing to a beat where he NAMES the tool.  Every tool this take names \u2014 Cursor, Google Workspace, Gmail, Google Calendar, Google Drive, Google Sheets \u2014 is a STAGE subject mark, and ROUND-2/3 law 6 keeps the story's own subject mark out of the depth field (the run-9 defect); the plan says in as many words that all six are deliberately ABSENT from the lanes.  Not one of the six depth marks is ever spoken, so a card carrying one would pop a live app window for a tool the viewer has not heard of, on a beat about somebody else.  The plan declares no pop-behind window (`per_lane_notes.cutout_tiktok`: 'the plan spends no beat on one') and the plan is the contract.  The handoff's alternative \u2014 spending the format's best detail on the chapter-1 tile arrivals at 7.70 / 8.86 / 10.76 \u2014 would put a SUBJECT mark on a card crossing a depth lane, which is the same violation from the other end.  Logged in plans/cursorworkspace_cutout_notes.md.",
